-- Passage rg10-theme-summary-p01
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('92c764c7-3bff-5062-a5e9-22529b0ba6d9', 'rg10-theme-summary-p01', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-theme-summary'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Rain Gauge$q$, $q$Every morning at seven, Imani Okafor walked to the end of her aunt's field and read the rain gauge. The volunteer weather network had asked for one number a day, written in a paper log and typed into a website by Sunday night. In June the job felt important. By August it felt pointless.

"Nobody is going to notice two millimeters," she told her aunt Ruth one dry morning. "Real scientists have satellites."

Ruth was pulling weeds from the bean rows and did not look up. "Then skip a day and see who notices."

Imani skipped three. Nothing happened. No one called, and the website did not complain. She felt a small, guilty relief, and then she went back to the gauge anyway, mostly out of habit.

In October a letter came from the network's regional coordinator, a hydrologist named Dr. Peter Lindqvist. He thanked the volunteers and included a map. A storm in September had dropped heavy rain in a narrow band across the county, and the official stations, which stood miles apart, had missed most of it. The map showed the band anyway, traced by dozens of small dots. Each dot was a backyard gauge. One of them sat in Ruth's field.

Imani found her own number in the table: forty-one millimeters on September 18. Below the table, the letter explained that the county had used the map to decide which drainage culverts to widen before winter.

That evening she opened the log to the three blank days in August. She could not fill them in, and she did not try. Instead she drew a small box around them in pencil, so she would remember what an empty space looked like. Then she set her alarm for seven.$q$, 'draft')
