-- Passage rg10-theme-summary-p03
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('449bc0cf-0735-56ec-90ec-03e29a31c04d', 'rg10-theme-summary-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-theme-summary'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Every Word$q$, $q$Mateo Reyes had been translating for his father since he was nine years old. He had done it at the doctor's office, at parent conferences, and once, memorably, at the counter of a tire shop. By fifteen he was quick and confident, and he took pride in making his father sound like someone who belonged in any room.

On Tuesday they sat across from Ms. Halvorsen, a loan officer at the bank on Fourth Street. His father wanted to borrow money for a second oven so the family bakery could fill larger orders for restaurants. Ms. Halvorsen had a stack of forms and a pen she clicked while she read.

"Why should we feel confident about this business?" she asked.

Mateo's father answered in Spanish for almost two minutes. He talked about waking at three every morning for eleven years. He named the regulars who came in before work: the nurse who wanted her rolls warm and the crossing guard who always paid in coins. He described the recipe for conchas that his own father had written on the back of a calendar page.

Mateo turned to Ms. Halvorsen. "He says business is steady."

She nodded and clicked her pen. His father watched her face, then looked at Mateo. He understood more English than he spoke.

"Tell her everything," he said quietly. "Every word."

Mateo's ears burned. He started over, slowly this time, searching for English words for things he had only ever heard in Spanish. He described the dark mornings, the nurse, the coins, the calendar page. Halfway through, Ms. Halvorsen set her pen down. When he finished, she asked whether the recipe still existed. His father smiled and said it was framed above the register.

She did not promise anything. She said she would call within a week.

On the walk home, Mateo replayed the meeting. For years he had believed his job was to make his father sound impressive. Now he wondered in how many rooms he had translated his father into someone smaller.$q$, 'draft')
