-- Passage rg10-theme-summary-p06
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('00c6ffa6-8e81-5edd-900b-2efbb206d8e7', 'rg10-theme-summary-p06', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-theme-summary'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Second Chair$q$, $q$For two years, Priya Raman had sat in the first cello chair of the Westbrook Youth Orchestra. Then, in September, a new student named Yusuf Adeyemi auditioned, and on Monday the seating chart showed his name in her place.

Priya told herself it did not matter. She told her mother it did not matter. Then she spent the week practicing with her door closed, playing the opening of the winter concert symphony so loudly that her little brother complained through the wall.

On Thursday Yusuf found her in the hallway before rehearsal. He held his music folded open to the fast passage in the third movement. "Mr. Castellanos takes this faster than my old conductor," he said. "I keep rushing it, and the whole section follows me right off the edge. You always land it. How do you count it?"

Priya almost said she was busy. Instead she showed him. She counted in groups of three rather than six, and she watched the violas instead of the conductor, because the violas always arrived a half beat early to warn you. Yusuf tried it twice and grinned.

At rehearsal, the passage came out clean for the first time all month. Mr. Castellanos stopped the orchestra and pointed his baton at the cellos. "That," he said. "Whatever you did, keep doing it."

Nobody knew whose idea it had been, and Priya was surprised to find that she did not need them to know. She had spent a week listening to herself through a closed door. In one afternoon she had heard the section become one sound.

At the concert she sat in the second chair, one seat to Yusuf's left. When the third movement arrived, she breathed with him on the upbeat, and the whole row of cellos came in together.$q$, 'draft')
